.TH CRMEMPOOL_CREATE_FROM_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER 3 "2026-10-30" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Macros"
.SH NAME
crmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer \- create ranged pool from user buffer
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <ccollections/cmempool.h>

crmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer(
    buffer, buf_size, smallest_size_power_of_two,
    largest_size_power_of_two,
    number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two, fb_policy,
    single_threaded, err);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Creates a ranged memory pool on top of a buffer that the caller
provides, so no sub-pool takes its storage from the heap. Fallback
entries, when the policy allows them, come from the standard malloc
and free.

It is the same as calling
crmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer_mp() with NULL memory
management procedures, and every rule of that page applies.
.fi
.SH PARAMETERS
.nf
buffer                                      Pointer to preallocated buffer
buf_size                                    Size of buffer in bytes (must
                                            match exactly what
                                            CRMEMPOOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER
                                            produces for the same
                                            parameters)
smallest_size_power_of_two                  Exponent for smallest pool size
largest_size_power_of_two                   Exponent for largest pool size
number_of_smallest_size_elems_power_of_two  Exponent for smallest pool count
fb_policy                                   Fallback policy for exhausted pools
single_threaded                             If true, take no lock
err                                         Optional pointer that receives
                                            an error string
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
Returns a pointer to the new ranged memory pool on success
Returns NULL on failure
.fi
.SH NOTES
.nf
The caller must destroy the pool with crmempool_destroy() before the
buffer goes out of scope.
.fi
.SH "SEE ALSO"
.BR CRMEMPOOL_DECLARE_PREALLOCATED_BUFFER (3),
.BR crmempool_create_from_preallocated_buffer_mp (3),
.BR crmempool_create (3),
.BR crmempool_destroy (3)
